<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#germanisation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanisation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Germanization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germanisation is the spread of the German language, people and culture into other lands, through settlement or state policy. In modern history the term refers above all to Prussian and German policies in Polish lands from the 1830s to 1918, including language laws and the Settlement Commission founded in 1886, and to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s brutal plans for occupied eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanisation.jpg" alt="Germanisation" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hstoops · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Conservatism_Flags_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
